{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 32, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a Crusader nobleman and one of the most prominent figures in the history of the County of Tripoli during the High Middle Ages. He became the ruler of Tripoli in 1152 after his father Pons succeeded Raymond II without having sons Raymond inherited the title and began a reign marked by military campaigns political intrigues and cultural patronage. His life and actions are mainly documented through chroniclers like William of Tyre and Imad ad-Din al-Isfahani providing insights into the complex dynamics of the Crusader states and the Muslim world of his time.\n\n*highlighted section part 1: Early Life and Background*\nRaymond III was born around 1115 to Pons Count of Tripoli and Cecilia of France who was a daughter of King Philip I of France. His family was part of the House of Toulouse which held significant influence in the Crusader states. Raymond’s upbringing was shaped by the turbulent environment of the Levant where Christian and Muslim powers frequently clashed. His education likely included training in both military skills and governance preparing him for his future role as a count.\n\nRaymond’s accession to the throne in 1152 was not immediate as his father Pons died around 1137 but Raymond was still young and the county was governed by regents. His uncle Raymond of Poitiers initially acted as regent until Raymond came of age. By the time Raymond took full control of the county he had gained valuable experience navigating the political landscape of Outremer.\n\n*highlighted section part 2: Reign and Military Campaigns*\nRaymond III’s reign was characterized by his efforts to expand Tripoli’s influence and secure its borders. One of his most notable military engagements was the siege of Damascus in 1148 which he joined as part of the Second Crusade. Although the crusaders ultimately failed to capture the city Raymond’s participation demonstrated his commitment to the defense and expansion of Christian territories in the region. His military strategies often involved alliances with other Crusader states and local Christian factions to counter the growing power of Muslim leaders like Nur ad-Din.\n\nIn addition to his military efforts Raymond III was involved in the political affairs of the Crusader states. He frequently corresponded and allied with other rulers such as Baldwin III of Jerusalem and Amalric I of Jerusalem. These alliances were crucial in maintaining a united front against the advancing Muslim forces led by figures like Saladin. Raymond also engaged in diplomacy with Muslim leaders attempting to secure treaties and truces to provide respite from constant warfare.\n\n*highlighted section part 3: Cultural Patronage and Legacy*\nBeyond his military and political endeavors Raymond III was known for his patronage of the arts and culture. His court at Tripoli was a center of learning and refinement where scholars poets and artisans gathered. He supported the construction and decoration of churches and monasteries contributing to the cultural heritage of the Crusader states. His influence extended to the promotion of Latin culture and the translation of important texts which helped bridge the gap between the Christian and Muslim worlds.\n\nRaymond III died in 1187 during the chaos following the Battle of Hattin where the Crusader forces suffered a devastating defeat by Saladin’s army. His death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli which subsequently faced increasing pressure from Muslim powers. Despite the eventual fall of the county Raymond’s reign is remembered for his contributions to the defense of the Crusader states his diplomatic skills and his efforts to maintain a balance between military action and cultural exchange in a time of great turmoil.\n\nHis legacy is also tied to the broader history of the Crusades providing insight into the complexities of living and ruling in a multi-cultural and often hostile environment. Chroniclers of the time painted him as a noble and capable leader whose actions reflected the best intentions of the Crusader nobility even as they grappled with the challenges of their era.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.06853077560663223}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -0.00012003655137959868}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.006313735153526068}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.29494979977607727}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.00038413776201196015}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -0.01811952330172062}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.4808386564254761}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.10021013766527176}, {"token": " Crus", "logprob": -1.3133610486984253}, {"token": "ader", "logprob": -0.000120751719805412}, {"token": " noble", "logprob": -0.46987926959991455}, {"token": "man", "logprob": -0.11176177859306335}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.7148820161819458}, {"token": " one", "logprob": -0.9779523611068726}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.0031443224288523197}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.0007996936910785735}, {"token": " most", "logprob": -0.1689135581254959}, {"token": " prominent", "logprob": -0.2040172517299652}, {"token": " figures", "logprob": -0.6200436353683472}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.2572554349899292}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.023546593263745308}, {"token": " history", "logprob": -1.3348195552825928}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -5.721882189391181e-05}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.005926417652517557}, {"token": " County", "logprob": -0.22493989765644073}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.537788011773955e-05}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -0.006554059684276581}, {"token": " during", "logprob": -0.6183958053588867}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.005611263681203127}, {"token": " High", "logprob": -0.9115464091300964}, {"token": " Middle", "logprob": -0.04367969185113907}, {"token": " Ages", "logprob": -5.221230458118953e-05}, {"tok